data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_0efe856612034a10006a5076cabe708196856e6a2bdd61d6de","logprobs":[],"output_index":6,"sequence_number":366,"text":"Top pick (best two-person tent under $300)\n- SkyView 2‑Person Tent — about $200, full feature set and very good value【8:1†source】.\n\nWhy I recommend it\n- Weather protection: waterproof materials with a included rainfly and double-stitched seams for reliable three‑season use【8:1†source】.  \n- Room + storage: comfortably fits two people and includes two vestibules for gear storage plus multiple interior pockets and a gear loft【8:1†source】.  \n- Ventilation: mesh panels/windows to reduce condensation and improve airflow【8:1†source】.  \n- Doors and access: two large doors for easy entry/exit【8:1†source】.  \n- Setup and hardware: freestanding design with lightweight aluminum poles, color‑coded setup, stakes, guylines and carrying bag included【8:1†source】【8:4†source】.  \n- Portability & weight: packs compact and is about 8 lb packed — practical for car camping and short backpacking trips【8:1†source】.  \n- Night visibility & durability extras: reflective guy lines and reinforced stake points【8:1†source】.\n\nReal-user notes / caveats\n- Reviewers praise the tent’s spaciousness, ventilation and waterproofing, but a few reported occasional zipper issues (minor)【8:2†source】.\n\nRunner-up (if you want alternatives)\n- Alpine Explorer 2‑person (three‑season) — noted for very fast setup and extras like a room divider and gear loft; intended more for car/family camping than heavy‑weather use【8:3†source】.\n\nIf you want, I can:\n- Compare these two head‑to‑head (weight, packed size, floor area), or  \n- Find similarly priced tents optimized for backpacking (lighter) or for storm/winter resistance (heavier, more durable). Which would you prefer?"}

